  "economic_rationale": "Multi-horizon construction: mispricings operate at distinct horizons with distinct decay profiles. SHORT (1M) captures overreaction reversal (Jegadeesh 1990, Lehmann 1990); MID (3-6M) captures gradual information under-reaction momentum (Jegadeesh-Titman 1993, Hong-Stein 1999); LONG (12M+) captures slow fundamental mean-reversion via value + quality (Fama-French 1993, Asness-Frazzini-Pedersen 2019 QMJ). Because these signals decay on different clocks, a horizon-conditional blend diversifies SIGNAL DECAY rather than just cross-sectional dispersion: IC of the blend can be steadier than any single horizon when sleeves are weakly correlated. The construction angle is the horizon MIX (decay-aware ICIR weighting), not a new factor family. Central validation: blend must beat the best single horizon sleeve (ICIR), else the mix adds nothing.",
